  <h2 class="subhead" id="Algorithm">Algorithm</h2>

    This algorithm needs to allow for arbitrary upsampling and downsampling of the input image on both the horizontal and vertical axis. 
    It also must be able to change the sampling ratio dynamically. <br> <br>
    
    With this algo, the coordinates are translated between source and destination images. Then the destination pixels 
    are computed as the average of all the overlapping pixels in the source domain. This Algo is formalized below. <br> <br>
  
    <br> 
    The input/output of this module are essentially the two below arrays:

<pre><code class="language-bash">input source_frame[w-1:0][h-1:0] = {0xII}           // Intensity value 0-255
output destination_frame[28-1:0][28-1:0] = {0xII}   // Intensity value 0-255
</code></pre>


    The source frame can be shown instead as the below continuous function: <br>
      $$ f(x, y) = source\_continuous(x, y) = source\_frame[floor(x)][floor(y)],  \{0 \le x < w, 0 \le y < h\}$$

    Coordinates can be translated from the destination to source domain with the below equation:
      <!-- $$ x_{src} = w*i/28 $$ -->
      <!-- $$ y_{src} = h*j/28 $$ -->
      $$ \vec{R_{src}}(i, j) = \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} w/28 & 0 \\ 0 & h/28 \end{bmatrix} * \begin{bmatrix} i \\ j \end{bmatrix} $$

    Where w, h are the width/height of the source pixel array respectively. And 28 is constant as the output frame is always 28x28. <br> <br>

    So the precise equation used for resizing is the below:
      $$ dest\_array[i][j] = \frac{\int_{A} f(x, y) dxdy}{\int_{A} dxdy}$$

    Where A is the area in between the points (i, j) -> (i + 1, j + 1) translated into the source domain. i.e. Area between two points below :
      $$ A(i, j):  \vec{R_{src}}(i, j) - to - \vec{R_{src}}(i + 1, j + 1) $$

    Given that the image may be upsampled or downsampled, the domain A may have widths/heights less than 1 (upsampled) or more than 1 (downsampled) <br> <br>

    If this project was to be done again, this module can likely be simplified a lot without sacrificing much functionality. 
    This could include only allowing downsampling, or making the output image classification size 32x32 rather than 28x28 to
    simplify some computations, or using a different resizing algo completely <br> <br>













    <h2 class="subhead" id="coefficients">Coefficient Update FSM</h2>
      This logic is used to generate the dynamic coefficients used by the resizing pipeline. 

      These coefficients need to be updated when the input box coordinates change.

      Update time is much less than 1 frame. <br> <br>

      These coefficients are calculated outside of the pipeline to reduce computations needed to be done in the pipeline and
      avoid having to divide, which is a many cycle operation. <br> <br>


      The coefficient FSM captures input coefficients, sequences the setup + capture of DSP/Divider hardware using these values, then updates
      the signals going to the pipeline. 
      It also synchronizes the updating of the coefficients with the end of the current frame, so they will 
      take effect at the start of the next one. <br> <br>

      High level diagram of this module:
      <p><img src="/imgs/resizing-coefficent-update-diagram.png" alt="img" width="50%" height="auto"></p>

      Description of generated coefficients:
      <pre><code class="language-bash">
topl[31:0] = { top_left_y[15:0], top_left_x[15:0] }     // coordinates of top-left corner to crop = (y1, x1)
botr[31:0] = { bot_right_y[15:0], bot_right_x[15:0] }   // coordinates of bottom-right corner to crop = (y2, x2)

width[23:0] = x2 - x1 + 1           // Width of the cropped image. Fixed-point 16b.8b value
height[23:0] = y2 - y1 + 1          // Height of the cropped image. Fixed-point 16b.8b value

dx[23:0] = width/28                 // How many source pixels map to one destination pixel. 16b.8b value. Used in horizontal average filter.
                                    // Maximum value = 1280p/28 ~= 45.714, Minimum value = 1/28 ~= 0.03571
dy[23:0] = height/28                // How many source pixels map to one destination pixel. 16b.8b value. Used in vertical average filter.
                                    // Maximum value = 720p/28 ~= 25.714, Minimum value = 1/28 ~= 0.03571

factor_w = 28/width                 // How many destination pixels map to one source pixel. 16b.8b value. Used in horizontal normalization
factor_h = 28/height                 // How many destination pixels map to one source pixel. 16b.8b value. Used in vertical normalization
      </code></pre>
      












  <h2 class="subhead" id="pipeline">Core 7-Stage Pipeline</h2>

    <img src="/imgs/pipeline.png" alt="img" width="50%" height="auto">
    
    <!----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------->
    <h3>1. Sub-image filter (Crop) </h3>
      Only pass pixels which lie in the bounds of the given box.
      <p style="color: red;">
      >> Latency impact: 0 // unused pixels are just skipped. <br>
      >> Area impact: ~37 registers, minimal gates // State and counter logic <br>
      </p>

    <!----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------->
    <h3>2. Luminance filter </h3>
      Convert the RGB pixel value into brightness value: 0.299*R + 0.587*G + 0.114*B
      <p style="color: red;">
      >> Latency impact: 3 pclk cycles // 3 cycles given for 8-bit mult + add operation<br>
      >> Area impact: ~50 registers from 3 DSP units imlpemented using logic gates<br>
      </p>

  <!----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------->
  <h3> FIFO stage </h3>
    Adding timing allowance for next stage. 64 Pixels/Bytes deep.
    <p style="color: red;">
    >> Latency impact: ~3 pclk cycles<br>
    >> Area impact: 64 deep x 8-bits/pixel <br>
    </p>

  <!----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------->
  <h3>3. Horizontal Average Filter </h3>
    Sum pixels in the horizontal direction. Output 1 pixel for every "dx" input pixels. 
    "dx" is a fixed-point value that may be less than or greater than 1. So the sum may start + end at a fractional pixel value. <br> <br>

    Features of this module include:
    <ul>
      <li>FSM to keep track of source + destination pixel domain left/right coordinates</li>
      <li>Single running sum which is accumulated with a MAC/DSP unit. So for each input pixel:  sum += pixel_value * overlap, where overlap is  between 0 and 1. </li>
      <li>Pixel sum outputs are passed on with hsync/vsync/valid signals. All sums are in 16b.8b fixed-point format.</li>
    </ul>

    Note on precision:
    <ul>
      <li>Max value a counter may reach is when dx = 1280p/28, so max count = 1280p/28 * 255 ~= 11660 (downsampling 1280 pixel row into 28 pixel row). So need >= 14 integer bits.</li>
      <li>Precision was chosen based on worst case scenarios like when dx = 1/28, and a pixel has an intensity of 0x1, in which case 
    the output pixel here should be incremented by 0.035714, which would be represented as 0x9 with 8 fractional bits. i.e. pretty okay.</li>
    </ul>

    <p style="color: red;">
      >> Latency impact: From last pixel input, 2 pclk cycles. Worst case limitation due to throughput per horizontal pixel row = ~ 64 pclk cycles<br>
      >> Area impact: ~286 registers <br>
    </p>

  <!----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------->
  <h3>4. Normalizer 1</h3>
    Normalize the pixels. All pixels should be normalized by factor of "factor_w"=1/"dx". <br>
    So if you added up "dx" pixels, you multiply by 1/"dx" to get the average. i.e. 1 half of the integral shown at the top of this page.
    <p style="color: red;">
      >> Latency impact: ~4 pclk cycles<br>
      >> Area impact: ~ 1 DSP unit <br>
    </p>

  <!----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------->
  <h3> FIFO stage </h3>
    Adding timing allowance for next stage. 32 Pixels/Bytes deep
    <p style="color: red;">
      >> Latency impact: Worst case ~32 pclk cycles<br>
      >> Area impact:  32 deep x 8-bits/pixel  <br>
    </p>
  
  <!----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------->
  <h3>5. Vertical Average Filter </h3>
    Sum pixels in the vertical direction. Output 1 pixel row for every "dy" input pixel rows. <br>
    28 counters are maintained at the same time. Once a row is finished, all 28 pixels are output sequentially
    and the next row is started. Fractional start/stop positioning is maintained.    <br> <br>
    This is split into 3 main sub-modules:
    <ul>
      <li>FSM for reading input pixels and tracking source + destination pixel coordinates.</li>
      <li>Pixel buffer for 28 counters, along with a Multiply+Add DSP unit which all counters share.</li>
      <li>FSM for sending out finished counts.</li>
    </ul>
    Otherwise operation is similar to horizontal average filter.
    <ul>
      <li>For each pixel: sum += pixel_value * overlap, where overlap is  between 0 and 1.</li>
      <li>Pixel outputs are 16b.8b unsigned fixed-point format.</li>
      <li>vsync / valid signals are maintained. </li>
    </ul>

    <p style="color: red;">
      >> Latency impact: Worst case ~10 pclk cycles from when last pixel on a row is input, to when first of 28 pixels is output. 28 pclk cycles to output a row.<br>
      >> Area impact: ~1300 registers + 1 MAC DSP. Including from 1 32x8-bit SRAM buffer for pixels. 1 32x24-bit for counters.<br>
    </p>

    <!------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------>
  <h3>6. Normalizer 2</h3>
    Normalize the sums done in vertical average filter. All pixels should be normalized by factor of "factor_h"=1/"dy". <br>
    Output of this block is an 8-bit pixel value, ready for use in K-Means classification.
    <p style="color: red;">
      >> Latency impact: ~4 pclk cycles<br>
      >> Area impact: ~ 1 DSP unit <br>
    </p>

  <h3>7. Stream to MM</h3>
    Write the 28x28 pixel stream into an SRAM buffer. Then signal to K-Means block to start the classification.












  <h2 class="subhead" id="verif">Verification Environment + Results</h2>
  
    This testbench reads a 1280x720p image from a memory file and generates an RGB video
    stream to be provided to the DUT, repeating this image for every video frame.  <br><br>

    At the same time, it reads a test vector file with a list of
    x1/y1/x2/y2 coordinates to be provided to the DUT on each subsequent frame. 
    Each time the box coordinates are changed, the internal coefficients will all be updated. And a new output image is generated. <br><br>
  

    The testbench will also capture every 28x28 output frame from the DUT and save it to a hex dump file for each sub-box input.
    After the test, results can be verified visually. <br><br>

    Using python scripts, the inputs/output can be converted between .hex + .png files. <br><br>
    
    If this project was done again, for more robust verification, support for many image files should be added, and results
    can be compared against a python implementation (including some margin for error due to fixed-point quantization.) <br><br>


